- How does the approach change if teleports have costs or limited usage per row/col?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial DP with a non-additive cost function and reconstruction, plus a precomputation layer for inversions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix length i and blocks t with transition from a previous breakpoint j
- invCost(l,r) as block cost (inversions within subarray)
- Reconstruction pointers for block boundaries
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk) or O(nk) with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function precomputeInv(a){
  const n=a.length; const inv=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let cnt=0;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      for(let i=l;i<r;i++) if(a[i]>a[r]) cnt++;
      inv[l][r]=cnt;
    }
  }
  return inv;
}
```

