- How would you optimize with divide-and-conquer DP or Knuth-like optimizations if invCost satisfies appropriate properties?
- How would you adapt to allowing at most k blocks or restoring the actual blocks efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests minimax-style DP with reconstruction for a classic game.

## Key Concepts
- Two-player DP with difference state
- Subarray DP and reconstruction path
- Time: O(n^2) space: O(n^2)

## Code Example
```javascript
// dp[i][j] denotes max diff for i..j
function maxFirstPlayer(values){
  const n = values.length; const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  let total = values.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][i]=values[i];
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0;i+len-1<n;i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(values[i]-dp[i+1][j], values[j]-dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return (total + dp[0][n-1])/2;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact L/R sequence from the DP table?
- How does this change if moves include taking from both ends with a coin flip to decide who starts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete DP skills on string partitioning with reconstruction; approachable for beginners yet non-trivial.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: maximum sum for first i digits split into t parts
- val(j,i): integer value of s[j..i-1] (leading zeros allowed)
- transition: dp[i][t] = max_{j ∈ [t-1..i-1]} dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i)
- reconstruction: track prev to recover split indices
- complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space; use BigInt for large results

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSplit(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = (a,b)=> BigInt(s.slice(a,b));
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1n));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    dp[i][1] = val(0,i);
  }

  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i);
        if(candidate > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct splits
  const splits = [];
  let curr = n, parts = k;
  while(parts > 1){
    const j = prev[curr][parts];
    splits.push(j);
    curr = j;
    parts--;
  }
  splits.reverse();

  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], splits};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical DP with fixed-length window constraints and reconstruction, mirroring analytics batching scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with exact segment count
- Prefix sums for fast interval sums
- Reconstruction path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithBounds(A, k, L, R){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const neg = -1/0; const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(neg));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for(let len=L; len<=R && len<=i; len++){
        if (dp[i-len][t-1] !== neg) {
          const val = dp[i-len][t-1] + (P[i]-P[i-len]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = len; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], take}
}
```

